.Dd August 20, 2026
.Dt CAIRN-API 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cairn-api
.Nd HTTP interface served by cairnd
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr cairnd 8
serves the contents of ZIM archives over a small local HTTP API.
It returns stored entries and metadata.
It does not render a website, rewrite HTML, inject a reader, or present a
catalogue.
The intended consumers are other programs.
.Pp
The interface is HTTP/1.1 over a unix socket or a loopback TCP address.
No other version is accepted: an
.Ql HTTP/1.0
or
.Ql HTTP/2
request line is answered
.Ql 505 .
No endpoint accepts a request body; a non-zero
.Ql Content-Length
is refused with
.Ql 400
before routing.
Only
.Ql GET
and
.Ql HEAD
are answered.
.Pp
Metadata is JSON; entry content is the bytes stored in the archive, unmodified.
.Sh ENDPOINTS
All endpoints live under
.Pa /v1 .
.Ss GET /v1/status
Daemon state, and the confinement that was actually applied.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "uptime_seconds": 3600,
  "listener": "unix:/run/cairn/cairn.sock",
  "archives": 2,
  "auth": "none",
  "sandbox": {
    "required": true,
    "layers": [
      {"name": "no_new_privs", "state": "applied", "detail": null},
      {"name": "landlock", "state": "applied", "detail": "abi 5"},
      {"name": "seccomp", "state": "applied", "detail": "49 syscalls, ..."}
    ]
  },
  "cache": {"budget_bytes": 67108864, "bytes": 0, "entries": 0,
            "hits": 0, "misses": 0, "evictions": 0},
  "connections": {"max": 64, "active": 1, "served": 0, "rejected": 0},
  "limits": {"max_request_line": 8192, "max_header_bytes": 16384,
             "max_headers": 64, "max_path_bytes": 1024,
             "suggest_max_query": 128, "suggest_max_results": 32}
}
.Ed
.Pp
A layer's
.Ql state
is
.Ql applied ,
.Ql unsupported ,
.Ql failed ,
or
.Ql disabled .
A daemon that failed to confine itself otherwise looks identical to one that
succeeded, which is why this is published rather than logged once at startup.
.Ss GET /v1/archives
Every open archive.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"archives": [
  {"uuid": "...", "title": "Wikipedia", "entry_count": 6000000,
   "cluster_count": 90000, "main_page": "index.html",
   "format_version": "6.3", "content_namespace": "C",
   "suggest": true}
]}
.Ed
.Pp
This is the entire discovery surface.
It is not a feed, and there is no catalogue protocol.
.Ss GET /v1/archives/{uuid}
One archive, plus its
.Ql M
namespace metadata.
Text values appear under
.Ql metadata ;
names whose value is binary or larger than
.Ql max_metadata_bytes
are listed under
.Ql binary_metadata .
.Ss GET /v1/archives/{uuid}/entry/{path}
Entry content, exactly as stored.
Redirects are followed, to a fixed small depth.
The response carries:
.Bl -tag -width "Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy" -compact
.It Ql Content-Type
The archive's MIME type, if it is a clean
.Ql type/subtype
token; otherwise
.Ql application/octet-stream .
.It Ql X-Cairn-Archive
The archive uuid, echoed from the request.
.It Ql X-Cairn-Path
The path after redirect following, percent-encoded.
A client can tell a redirect target from a direct hit without a second request.
.It Ql Accept-Ranges
Always
.Ql bytes .
.It Ql X-Content-Type-Options
Always
.Ql nosniff .
.It Ql Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy
Always
.Ql same-origin .
.It Ql Content-Security-Policy
From
.Ql content_security_policy
in
.Xr cairn.conf 5 .
.El
.Ss HEAD /v1/archives/{uuid}/entry/{path}
Size and type without the body.
.Ql Content-Length
describes the entry that would be sent.
Note that computing it costs the same work as the
.Ql GET :
the length of a blob is only known once its cluster is decoded.
.Ss GET /v1/archives/{uuid}/random
One random entry path from the content namespace.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"archive": "...", "path": "Rhinoceros"}
.Ed
.Ss GET /v1/archives/{uuid}/suggest?q=PREFIX&limit=N
Title-prefix suggestions, from a binary search over the archive's title
pointer list.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"archive": "...", "suggestions": [{"title": "Apple", "path": "Apple"}]}
.Ed
.Pp
Suggestions come from the archive's own title ordering: the entry
.Pa X/listing/titleOrdered/v1
in archives written by a current libzim, or the header's title pointer list in
older ones.
The first covers front articles only, so a suggestion is always an article,
never a metadata or asset entry.
An archive carrying neither ordering answers with an empty list; the
.Ql suggest
field of
.Pa /v1/archives
says whether an archive can answer at all.
.Pp
Matching is byte-exact on the stored title: no case folding, no diacritic
folding.
.Ql q
is required and bounded by
.Ql suggest_max_query ;
.Ql limit
is clamped to
.Ql suggest_max_results .
There is no full-text search in cairn 1.x.
.Sh PATHS
.Ql {uuid}
is the canonical lowercase hyphenated form of the UUID stored in the archive
header.
Any other form is
.Ql 400 ,
not a normalization.
Ids are stable across restarts, renames and machines, and cannot be guessed
from a filename:
.Pa /v1/archives
is the only way in.
.Pp
.Ql {path}
is a key into the archive's table, not a filesystem path, so traversal cannot
escape.
It is percent-decoded exactly once.
.Ql %00 ,
raw control bytes, invalid escapes and non-canonical UTF-8 (over-long forms,
surrogates) are all
.Ql 400 .
A decoded result is never decoded again.
Everything else in the target is matched literally, including in the
.Ql {uuid}
segment.
.Pp
Lookup tries the archive's content namespace first:
.Ql C
in modern archives,
.Ql A
in older ones.
If that misses and the path looks like
.Ql N/rest
where
.Ql N
is a single namespace character, the explicit form is tried next.
That is what cross-namespace links in older archives look like.
Two probes, no scan.
.Sh RANGE REQUESTS
A single range only.
Multipart
.Ql byteranges
is refused with
.Ql 416 :
it is an amplification vector and a parser surface for no benefit to the
intended clients.
A satisfiable range is
.Ql 206
with
.Ql Content-Range ;
an unsatisfiable one is
.Ql 416
with
.Ql Content-Range: bytes */LENGTH .
A syntactically invalid
.Ql Range
header is ignored and the whole entry is sent.
.Sh AUTHENTICATION
If
.Ql auth_token
is configured, every request needs
.Ql Authorization: Bearer TOKEN ,
compared in constant time.
The check happens
.Em before
routing, so an unauthenticated client cannot tell a missing archive from a
present one: everything is
.Ql 401 .
There are no accounts, no sessions, and no roles.
.Sh RENDERING ENTRIES IS THE CLIENT'S PROBLEM
cairn returns archived HTML unmodified.
It does not rewrite links, strip scripts, or sanitize anything: that would put
an HTML parser in the hot path, and it will not be added later.
.Pp
A browser-based client that renders an entry is therefore rendering
attacker-controlled markup.
.Em Render entries in an isolated origin .
The
.Ql nosniff ,
.Ql same-origin
and
.Ql Content-Security-Policy
headers above are what a server can do without parsing content; they are not a
substitute for origin isolation.
This is a documented limitation, not a defect awaiting a sanitizer.
.Sh LIMITS
Every bound a client can push on is configurable and documented in
.Xr cairn.conf 5 ,
and none is unbounded: request line and header sizes, header count,
concurrent connections, read and write timeouts, keep-alive length,
per-connection request rate, decompressed cluster size, and cluster cache
budget.
Their live values are in
.Pa /v1/status .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ curl --unix-socket /run/cairn/cairn.sock http://cairn/v1/archives
$ cairn get 8b1f... index.html > index.html
$ curl -H 'Range: bytes=0-1023' --unix-socket /run/cairn/cairn.sock \e
    http://cairn/v1/archives/8b1f.../entry/video.webm
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
One shape, always:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"error": {"code": "not_found", "message": "no such resource"}}
.Ed
.Pp
The message is a fixed string.
Nothing the client sent is ever echoed back.
Codes are stable:
.Bl -column "range_not_satisfiable" "status" -compact -offset indent
.It Sy code Ta Sy status
.It Li bad_request Ta 400
.It Li bad_uuid Ta 400
.It Li bad_path Ta 400
.It Li bad_query Ta 400
.It Li body_not_allowed Ta 400
.It Li unauthorized Ta 401
.It Li not_found Ta 404
.It Li method_not_allowed Ta 405
.It Li request_timeout Ta 408
.It Li uri_too_long Ta 414
.It Li range_not_satisfiable Ta 416
.It Li too_many_requests Ta 429
.It Li headers_too_large Ta 431
.It Li internal Ta 500
.It Li archive_unavailable Ta 503
.It Li version_not_supported Ta 505
.El
.Pp
A malformed region inside one archive produces
.Ql 503
for that archive, not
.Ql 500
for the daemon, and never a panic.
A missing archive and a missing entry are both
.Ql not_found :
the difference is not worth leaking and not useful to a client.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cairn 1 ,
.Xr cairn.conf 5 ,
.Xr cairnd 8
.Sh STANDARDS
The ZIM format is specified by the openZIM project.
Where this page and the specification disagree, the specification wins.
.Sh CAVEATS
cairn 1.x has no full-text search.
That is a real capability gap against
.Xr kiwix-serve 1 ,
and closing it means either a Xapian reader in Rust or a sidecar index.
Title-prefix suggestion is what is offered instead.
